.TH NTPSYNC 1M "4 November 2026" "amix-base"
.SH NAME
ntpsync \- set the clock from time servers, at boot and hourly
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ntpsync
.RB [ \-b " | " \-c " | " \-n " | " \-i " | " \-l ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ntpsync
asks time servers for the time over SNTP, through
.BR rdate (1M),
sets the clock from the first good answer, and then sets the Amiga's
battery clock from it, so that a boot without the network still starts
close to the right time.
Nothing stays in memory between runs.
.PP
It runs at boot, from
.B /etc/init.d/ntp
.RB ( /etc/rc2.d/S77ntp ),
in the background: it waits there for
.BR ifup (1M)
to give an interface its address, so the boot never waits for it.
It then runs every hour from root's
.BR crontab (1),
at a minute picked once for each machine, so that machines do not all ask
at the same time.
.PP
The servers are asked in this order, each once:
the time servers the DHCP server named (option 42, recorded by
.BR dhcpc (1M)
in the leases it holds now; those of the interface
.B ifup
brought up first, first), then
.BR rc.conf 's
.BR ntp_servers .
Each server gets
.B ntp_timeout
seconds, the name lookup included, and
.B ntp_retries
more tries before the next server.
.PP
A sync is one line in
.BR /var/adm/rc.log ,
and so is a run that could not sync, whatever the number of servers
asked.
The hourly run writes a line only when the outcome changes (synced, then
not; not, then synced again), so a machine that stays off the network
writes one line, not one an hour.
.SH OPTIONS
With no option, sync now: the clock is stepped and the outcome is printed
as well as logged.
This works with
.B ntp=no
too.
.TP
.B \-b
the boot run: wait up to
.B ntp_wait
seconds for the network, then sync, stepping the clock.
.TP
.B \-c
the hourly run: sync, slewing an offset under a minute with
.BR adjtime (2)
instead of stepping, so that the clock never goes back: to lose time it
stands still until it is right, and to gain it runs fast; it prints
nothing.
.TP
.B \-n
say what would be done: whether the boot and hourly runs are on, the
servers in order, the
.B rdate
command, the hourly run's minute, and the last outcome.
.TP
.B \-i
put the hourly line in root's crontab, or take it out, as
.BR rc.conf 's
.B ntp
says; the boot step runs it.
.TP
.B \-l
print the boot run's time bound in seconds, for the boot step.
.SH "RC.CONF KEYS"
.TP
.BR ntp= yes | no
the boot and hourly runs; default
.BR yes .
With
.BR no ,
the next boot takes the hourly line out of root's crontab, and the hourly
run does nothing until then.
.TP
.BI ntp_servers= "\(dqname ...\(dq"
the servers, after DHCP's; default
.BR "0.pool.ntp.org 1.pool.ntp.org 2.pool.ntp.org" .
A name needs a name server
.RB ( interfaces (4)'s
.B dns
line or a DHCP server's), or a line in
.BR /etc/inet/hosts ;
an address needs neither.
A machine with no name server cannot look up the default names and is never
set: the log and
.B \-n
say so.
Add a
.B dns
line to
.BR /etc/inet/interfaces ,
or addresses to
.B ntp_servers
in
.BR /etc/rc.conf .
.TP
.BI ntp_timeout= seconds
the bound of one try at one server; default 15.
.TP
.BI ntp_retries= n
the tries after the first, at each server; default 1.
.TP
.BI ntp_wait= seconds
how long the boot run waits for the network; default 120, which is
.BR ifup 's
own bound.
.SH "THE BATTERY CLOCK"
After a sync,
.B ntpsync
waits for the start of the next minute and runs
.BR "setclk \-s" ,
which writes the system time to the battery clock in the zone
.B /etc/clkzone
names, to the minute.
The
.B setclk
of the stock system writes the wrong year after 1999 and is never run;
the line in the log says so, and the install's corrected
.B setclk
is the one used.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /etc/rc.conf
the keys above.
.TP
.BI /var/inet/dhcpc/ interface .lease
the DHCP server's time servers, in
.BR DHCP_NTP .
.TP
.B /var/inet/ifup.primary
the interface
.B ifup
brought up first; without it there is no network to ask.
.TP
.B /var/inet/ntp.state
the last outcome.
.TP
.B /var/adm/rc.log
one line for each outcome.
.TP
.B /var/adm/rclog/ntp
the boot run's output: each server that did not answer.
.TP
.B /usr/amiga/bin/setclk
writes the battery clock.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when the clock was set, and for
.B \-b
and
.B \-c
with
.BR ntp=no ;
1 otherwise.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
.ft CW
# /etc/rc.conf
ntp=yes          # set the clock from time servers at boot and hourly: ntpsync(1M)
ntp_servers="192.0.2.5 time.example.com"
.ft R
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dhcpc (1M),
.BR ifup (1M),
.BR rdate (1M),
.BR setclk (1M),
.BR crontab (1),
.BR rc.conf (4)
.SH NOTES
A machine whose clock is far off (a flat battery) is stepped at boot by
the whole difference, and so is one more than a minute off at an hourly
run.
Password ageing counts days from the clock: see
.BR shadow (4)
before setting a clock forward by hand.
